#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "Best use of a color checker with the Color Calibration module"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:21
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The original address was `https://community.ansel.photos/view-discussion/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color`.\n"
"You can check this copy against the [snapshot in the Internet Archive](https://web.archive.org/web/20231001133110/https://community.ansel.photos/view-discussion/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:28
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "*Posted by **migmoq** on 2023-05-18.*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:30
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I have a color checker. My question is what is the best use of it with the Color Calibration module :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:33
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- by shooting the color checker in a sunny day and then define a calibration preset in the Color Calibration module for all the images taken with the same camera\n"
"- by shooting the color checker during each of my photo sessions and then define a color calibration for only the images taken during this photo session\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:35
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In my case, I have some projects that take place over several months or years. And I have also, in parallel, some one-day shoot sessions. (Also the exact accuracy of the colors for a given session or projects are rare.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:41
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The profile of the camera depends on the light, the color of the iluminant.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:43
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The card should be used each time you change the illuminant.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:45
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The results would be different if you take it under a D65 light in controlled conditions, at the sun in a sunny day or in cloudy day or at sunset.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:47
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It may be even different under a sunny day in different months or at different places.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:49
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "ISo setting may also affect color captured by the camera\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:51
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "So you should use it at the beginning of the session or even more if you change the light.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:57
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "> by shooting the color checker in a sunny day and then define a calibration preset in the Color Calibration module for all the images taken with the same camera\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:59
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is recommended if you want to do a generic profile. This is what I use for street photography and candid shots taken outside, where I can't and don't want to carry a colorchecker to shoot on location. Let's be honest : it's cumbersome and overkill in most cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:61
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "> by shooting the color checker during each of my photo sessions and then define a color calibration for only the images taken during this photo session\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:63
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is recommended if you want to achieve maximum quality, for professional use. This is what I do for assignments, fashion/promotional shoots, anything planned ahead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:65
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The purpose of using color checkers reference shoot is to **speed up** the editing process by having a white balance/enhanced profile to automatically set color calibration. That would be when you have many pictures to edit, that were shot in the same setup, and you take a couple of reference color checker shots.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:67
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now, if every next picture on your memory card is a color checker reference shot because you change setups all the time, color checkhers are actually **slowing your process down** compared to color tuning visually/manually with sliders.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:69
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The best option is the fastest, except perhaps in those rare case where you may have colored bounced light that will give you an hard time with the manual approach (but the automated profile computing may still need to be manually adjusted later — use your best judgment).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:73
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**migmoq** — 2023-05-29\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/best-use-of-a-color-checker-with-the-color.md:75
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ok, thanks. Both approach is ok. It depends on the use cases. I have to target the more efficient way according to the context. I will set up a preset for sunny days and another one for cloudy ones for long-time projects (or for candid shots) to facilitate the post-processing. And I will define a color calibration for others photo sessions only when the color accuracy is required.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "Develop photo with flames"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:20
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The original address was `https://community.ansel.photos/view-discussion/develop-photo-with-flames`.\n"
"No crawler ever captured this particular thread, so the text below — taken straight from the forum database — is the only copy left.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:27
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "*Posted by **cendalc** on 2024-01-15.*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:29
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hi, I'd like to get some tips how to develop photo with flames (e.g. candle). I am not able to create vibrant yellow flames. The best I could have done is this:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:33
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Source RAW with my edits is on https://1drv.ms/f/s!AtyecmjANN7wna4lzFcpFDC4z_50eA?e=Jj9Bk7\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:35
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Any tips/help is much appreciated!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:39
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**cendalc** — 2024-01-15\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:41
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For another picture, as soon as I enable Filmic, the flames are red and I don't know what to do to fix it. Here is a snapshot between enabled/disabled Filmic.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:45
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Source RAW also posted to [https://1drv.ms/f/s!AtyecmjANN7wna4lzFcpFDC4z_50eA?e=Jj9Bk7](https://1drv.ms/f/s!AtyecmjANN7wna4lzFcpFDC4z_50eA?e=Jj9Bk7)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:51
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Red is actually radiometrically-accurate for flames, as in : it's consistent with the lightspectrum the camera recorded. Indeed, the color temperature of flames is actually… its own burning temperature (gas burns at high temperature with blue flame, coal and candles burn lower with red flames that you don't actually see red…). See here the \"theoritical\" color of illuminants in relation with temperature (in kelvin):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:55
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Filmic honors that and apply gamut mapping (aka desaturation) to fit colors into the display color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:57
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now, what you perceptually expect here is the [Bezold-Brücke shift](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bezold%E2%80%93Br%C3%BCcke_shift), aka a yellow shift of very bright red lights, but the camera is not subjected to it because it's a product of human retina.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:59
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What you see without filmic is the product of gamut clipping, where green clips earlier than red, producing a yellow shift by accident (along with ugly flat areas).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:61
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The solution is a mix :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:64
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"1.  in filmic options, go for \"color science\" v7,\n"
"2.  in color balance RGB, manually push highlights to yellow with the highlights gain setting (in 4 ways tab).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:66
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "More details:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:68
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=34iZotlYBBs\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:74
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Another note : you will not be able to get a lot of saturation on colors that are very bright. That is a limitation of RGB color spaces. This is the sRGB gamut on a lightness, chroma graph at constant hue :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/develop-photo-with-flames.md:78
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You see that at 100% lightness (top tip), you can only have white. The pure yellow tip is probably at 80 % lightness. So if you want saturation in flames, you will need to darken them so they don't end up at the white end of the dynamic range, because there is no room for saturation in there.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/community/help-with-interface.md:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "help with interface"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/help-with-interface.md:20
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The original address was `https://community.ansel.photos/view-discussion/help-with-interface`.\n"
"No crawler ever captured this particular thread, so the text below — taken straight from the forum database — is the only copy left.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/help-with-interface.md:29
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hi there, first, Ansel is great, much faster and pleasant to use on older systems than DT. But there are some things that really hold me adopting Ansel as my \"go to raw processing app\":\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/help-with-interface.md:33
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- I totally understand the focus on the pipeline, but when a search for a module, the pipeline tab rolls to the left and get lost on that tab bar. I think that pipeline tab should be fixed on the left corner. It will save some time, mouse wheel rolls and clicks; (and yes, i read the article about removing the old iconic tab, but... i'm used to customize interface options on software that allows changes to suit my needs, and had my \"favorites\" tab well balanced...)\n"
"- is there any way to resize the \"Display Metadata\" tab? for me, when enabled, it covers a fixed 3/4 of the left panel, and i cannot figure out how to resize it from the gui (i use the metadata to compare files in different folders, and enabling/disabling that module every time to change the active folder is annoying);\n"
"- i can't find a way to configure the default set of modules enabled for \"new\" raw files. Every first time a raw file is opened, Ansel enables: 1.original, 2.raw black/white point, 3.demosaic... yada yada... 12.orientation. I can't figure out how to change the order, neither the items on this default list. Is it possible btw? Creating a new set of default modules or change the stack order will speedup my workflow;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/help-with-interface.md:35
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "And, one more time: congratulations for Aurélien (and others here who can code) for this initiative, you guys rock!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/help-with-interface.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I really don´t know if my complaints are pathetic or important in any way, but they are coming from an inexperienced user, trying to leave adobe lightroom (for the average user, LR can do magic!).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/help-with-interface.md:39
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hugs from Brasil and sorry about my english mistakes (self taught)!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/help-with-interface.md:45
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hey Nirceu,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
