msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:28
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "*Posted by **Jofial** on 2023-03-22.*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:32
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I have a query. My photographic background comes from the days of chemical photography (not digital). We used to make use of toning to preserve prints (Selenium, platinum, sepia, etc.).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:34
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In DT I used to use the color correction module. Now obsolete in Ansel. This module had a virtue very appreciated by me because it allowed me to tacked the images without \"tinting\" the whites. In the current modules I have not been able to do this. They all end up \"coloring\" the whites. The tacking only attacked exposed and developed silver halides, the whites not having such halides, were not affected.  \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:42
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Scorpion078** — 2023-03-23\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:44
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "you can try applying a parametric mask where you protect the whites\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:50
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can tint shadows only in color balance RGB, using the \"4 channels tab\", and a mix of offset and shadows lift, possibly even the midtones power.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:54
#: content/community/toning-images.md:62
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Jofial** — 2023-03-23\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:56
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "But it is not the same in terms of operability. With the \"color correction\" it was two clicks: set the white in the center and move the black to one of the color/tone boxes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:58
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I will look for a solution and create a preset. I will comment if I find a good solution\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:64
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I will try it and let you know if it works for me.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:72
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The problem of color correction is the transition to black to white is done linearly in CIE Lab space, assuming a display-referred (bounded white) image. That assumption is voided in our pipeline.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:76
#: content/community/toning-images.md:90
#: content/community/toning-images.md:122
#: content/community/toning-images.md:134
#: content/community/toning-images.md:152
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Jofial** — 2023-03-24\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:78
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While trying to achieve a tonal shift using 4-way color balance, I noticed the following.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:80
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As I have not seen any reference in the Ansel or Darktable documentation, I ask if this is normal and what is the purpose of the percentage being so different between the different vias.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:82
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I leave an image\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:84
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Any ideas or advice?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:92
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To match the ease of converting my B&W photographs to a tonal tonal shift such as Sepia, Cyanotype, Platinum, Selenium, etc., I have to use a tonal shift to convert my B&W photographs to a tonal shift such as Sepia, Cyanotype, Platinum, Selenium, etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:94
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I have only found with this tool that it allows to be worked by independent channels and allows the \"scene mode\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:96
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Would it be acceptable in a scene mode workflow?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:104
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Yes, it's normal. The same number does not have the same effect depending on the setting, so the GUI has been adjusted for consistent visual effect. Numbers are just symbols, they don't mean anything in themselves.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:110
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Again, this is a GUI that expects 100% to sit at the far end of the graph, aka display-referred framework. It can work to some extend in scene-referred, but I don't see why you would want that for toning monochrome.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:116
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I wrote that just for you : https://ansel.photos/en/workflows/monochrome-toning/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:118
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It's work in progress, but the fundamentals are there.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:124
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thank you. I'm going to try, and check my obsession that the whites are not colored\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:130
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "They won't if you follow the guide.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:136
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Yes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:138
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I am very happy and embarrassed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/toning-images.md:140
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Happy, because now I have a method very similar to the chemical one without breaking the flow referred to the scene.\n"
