#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "Vibrant colors and skin tones"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:20
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The original address was `https://community.ansel.photos/view-discussion/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones`.\n"
"No crawler ever captured this particular thread, so the text below — taken straight from the forum database — is the only copy left.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:27
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "*Posted by **nicorikken** on 2024-11-12.*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:29
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I've been reading up on Ansel color dimensions to add more color to my event photo's without it looking too unnatural.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:31
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I fail to understand why 'basic colorfulness: natural skin' preset in Color balance rgb is defined using saturation instead of chroma. Chroma seems to me more fitting for skin tones. What was the thought process of this preset? (I did watch the videos on this module).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:33
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Also Color balance rgb sliders differentiate on luminance (shadows, midtones, highlights) and so doesn't account for skin tones as other software does in a 'vibrance' setting. I did some experiments using a parametric mask on hue, which works but also will take time to get to a good default setting. How do other users deal with this?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Pedro Rodríguez** — 2024-11-12\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:39
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Saturation is designed to avoid neon colors. If you increase chroma too much, you easily get colors that seem to glow. Saturation alleviates this by slightly darkening as you increase it, and slightly lightening as you decrease it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:41
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Masks are a good option, but skin is often in the midtones, so if you increase saturation in the midtones you often get good results. Also, I've been playing with the shadows and highlights falloff in the masks tab, as well as where both areas overlap.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:43
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once color equalizer is merged, you will be able to target specific hues much quicker, though.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:45
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hope that helps\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:49
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**nicorikken** — 2024-11-12\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:51
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thanks. I did some more experimentation and noticed that when I was pushing colors to extremes to exaggerate the effect, I never pushed chroma as much as saturation because the slider of chroma goes to 50% where the slider of saturation goes to 100%. I mistakenly assumed that saturation was resulting in a worse look. Going for a similar result using chroma by manually entering higher numbers results in brighter reds and blues that appear neon-like, as you mentioned. So saturation as implemented in the great color science in Ansel is the better way to go.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:53
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color equalizer would indeed be a great solution to be able to push saturation some more while avoiding the saturation effect on skin tones. Until then I can distinguish on luminance using the sliders or use filters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:55
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Of course the proper color calibration and lighting conditions also have an effect on saturation on skin tones. I have one photo where a few people have a warmer reflected light on them than the main people of interest. When pushing the saturation the warmer lit people become orange rather quickly compare to the ones for which the color correction is set.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/vibrant-colors-and-skin-tones.md:57
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If somebody else has tips or suggestions I'm happy to learn them.\n"
msgstr ""

