#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:43
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For example, Nvidia has the GeForce (gamers) and Quadro (pro) ranges, but they use the same chips. From what I understood, it seems GeForce are optimized for real-time rendering (high FPS in video games), while Quadro are optimized for offline rendering (3D rendering, machine-learning, etc.). Offline and real-time are different way to use the memory and lead to different optimization choices in the drivers (continuous I/O at low latency vs. timed I/O at high FLOPS).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:45
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Nonetheless, it seems the GeForce models consistently get better scores in OpenCL benchmarks… Go figure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:47
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You may find relevant benchmarks for OpenCL perf here :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:50
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- https://compubench.com/result.jsp\n"
"- https://browser.geekbench.com/opencl-benchmarks\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:52
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I would just pick the highest ranking model that your money can buy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:54
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you stumble upon other benchmarks and tests, be sure they test OpenCL, because the performance of DirectX, OpenGL, Vulkan, TensorFlow, etc. is mildly relevant for us here. Also, the trick is performance depends on the driver version too.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:56
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, there is a trick with GPU used for OpenCL : you don't need them connected to your screen. Say you have an integrated Intel chip or a weak discrete GPU, you can keep it to render anything graphic (OpenGL, desktop environment, etc.) and send the image buffer to the display via HDMI/VGA/DisplayPort. Then you can use your beefy GPU only through OpenCL.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:58
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I have seen benchmarks where offscreen GPU offers twice as much speed than what you get with them onscreen.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:62
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**migmoq** — 2023-11-24\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:64
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "I'm taking advantage of the question about OpenCL to ask an additional one: some GPU supports OpenCL 3 whereas others supports OpenCL 2.1.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:66
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What is the expectation of Ansel on the version of OpenCL?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/community/do-opencl-accelerated-modules-scale-with.md:72
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel uses the OpenCL 1.2 API. Note that I have seen benchmarks where some tasks take longer to complete with OpenCL 2 than 1, so it's not necessarily better. Also, the OpenCL versions are API versions, they mostly just add new methods and types to speed-up development, ultimately the performance depends how vendor implement the API.\n"
msgstr ""

